A block of code has been removed: 
```
		// Support opening foreign resources (such as a http link that points outside of the workbench)
		const resourceInput = <IResourceInput>input;
		if (resourceInput.resource instanceof URI) {
			const schema = resourceInput.resource.scheme;
			if (schema === Schemas.http || schema === Schemas.https) {
				window.open(resourceInput.resource.toString(true));

				return TPromise.wrap<IEditor>(null);
			}
		}
```
it used to be in the `WorkbenchEditorService` under `editor/commons`. Now it seems to be moved under `editor/browser` and class is EditorService

Here is the commit that introduced the regression: https://github.com/Microsoft/vscode/commit/ef92f85c06587053810e29b641f619021d62468c#diff-7260c109d203125646107c652086f25c

Our VSCode extension was relying on this behaviour to open URL in the browser for a workspace symbol with `Location` `uri` property being an `http`/`https` schema URL.

Are there any workarounds?
If not any chance this code can be put back or moved in here for example? https://github.com/Microsoft/vscode/blob/master/src/vs/workbench/browser/quickopen.ts#L276

- VSCode Version: 1.32.1
- OS Version: Windows 10 16299.1004

Steps to Reproduce:

1. Create a shell task in `tasks.json` supposed to run under WSL:
```json
{
    "label": "My Task",
    "options": {
      "shell": {
        "executable": "${env:windir}\\system32\\wsl.exe",
      }
    },
    "type": "shell",
    "command": "ls / && echo OK # comment",
    "problemMatcher": []
}
```
2. Run "My Task". It will fail with the following error: 
```
> Executing task: ls / && echo OK # comment <

/bin/bash: ls / && echo OK # comment: No such file or directory
The terminal process terminated with exit code: 127
```
Does this issue occur when all extensions are disabled?: Yes

This behavior is new for 1.32 (present in Insider 1.33). By tracing the process invocation, I can see that the `command` property is enclosed within double quotes, causing WSL to interpret the whole script as a command file. The `args` property doesn't work properly either, causing all arguments to be enclosed within double quotes.

A possible, but unpleasant, workaround is to put the whole command into the `args` property of the `shell` options:

```json
{
    "label": "My Task",
    "options": {
      "shell": {
        "executable": "${env:windir}\\system32\\wsl.exe",
        "args": [ "ls", "/", "&&", "echo", "OK", "#", "comment" ]
      }
    },
    "type": "shell",
    "command": "",
    "problemMatcher": []
}
```
